WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.060
- What is the church's status
on this public university?

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.970
And the answer is it
has no status whatsoever

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.340
because it's not connected
in any way, shape, or form

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.690
because it's on its own land

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.985
that is just nearby to
the public university.

00:00:12.985 --> 00:00:15.568
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.020
There's been a movie franchise

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.970
that I've been putting off reacting to

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.736
because of all of the theological

00:00:22.736 --> 00:00:24.420
and philosophical implications,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.957
so today we're gonna talk about,

00:00:25.957 --> 00:00:28.890
"God's Not Dead 3: A
Light in the Darkness,"

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.690
with none other than
Michael from Wisecrack.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.130
Thanks so much for being here, Michael.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.540
- I'm so glad to be here.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.880
If ever there is a movie I wanted to watch

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.590
and talk about with you,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.390
for whatever reason,
this would be the one.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.860
- All right, well, for better or worse,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.650
let's dig into "God's Not Dead 3."

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:44.670
- Oh, I can't wait.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.920
- We're seeing more and more
protests around the city

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.570
since local pastor David Hill

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.400
was arrested for contempt of court.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.870
- Hill refused to abide

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.915
by a controversial government mandate

00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:57.510
to turn over transcripts of
his sermons to city officials.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.930
- If we're looking at this news report

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.960
that is potentially a
summary from the Local News,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.317
that is the name of the website.

00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:08.100
"Reverend David Hill, pastor
of local St. James Church,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.210
was arrested Monday

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.590
after refusing to abide by

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.510
a controversial government mandate

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.730
for all Hope Springs religious leaders

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.050
to turn over their sermons

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.790
to government officials for review.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.040
Many are accusing the state
of overstepping its bounds"

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.337
Yeah, you think?
- Yeah.

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:24.780
- "claiming they are on
a witch hunt to silence"

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.613
To silent?

00:01:25.613 --> 00:01:26.610
- Religious leaders.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.957
- To silence religious leaders
is I think what they meant,

00:01:28.957 --> 00:01:31.860
"from making political
statements from the pulpit."

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.320
- They didn't grammar check
the Local News article.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.510
- I mean, this is why local news is dying.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.240
- I mean, doesn't that
violate some sort of church,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.073
state, religious
- [Devin] Yeah,

00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:41.370
- freedom distinction
- a million different things.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:44.100
So the only thing that this
could possibly be related

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.260
to is what's called the Johnson Amendment

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.680
which it's not a mandate against
churches being political.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.120
It is that if you
explicitly get into politics

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.393
then you potentially lose
your status as a nonprofit.

00:01:57.393 --> 00:01:58.226
- Okay. Yeah.

00:01:58.226 --> 00:01:59.059
- So factually

00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:00.930
everything the church does
would then be taxable.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:01.860
No one's going to,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.418
like the IRS police
aren't gonna come around

00:02:03.418 --> 00:02:05.946
and force you to turn over your sermon.

00:02:05.946 --> 00:02:07.800
That's just not a thing.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.900
- Wow. So I've learned a
lot and I've also learned

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.530
that there's no fact checker
at the Hope Springs Local News.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.460
- Now a contentious

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.400
and heated free speech debate
is threatening to boil over

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.983
while the pastor sits in
jail waiting for a ruling.

00:02:19.983 --> 00:02:23.520
(melancholic music)

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.123
- The fact that Ted McGinley
is in this too is crazy.

00:02:27.091 --> 00:02:30.330
(door buzzes)

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.920
- Let's go.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.170
- You know, I mean, it's almost like,

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.540
and I would never accuse
the filmmaker of this,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.130
they wanted to gin up
the idea that Christians

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.050
in America are under attack.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.380
- It's almost like all of
these movies are operating

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.790
with that fundamental assumption.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.720
- I don't think that's the case.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.280
I would never accuse
the filmmaker of that.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.200
It's the baggage that
I'm bringing into this

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.033
- Yes.

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:56.653
- that they're taking on
a persecution complex.

00:02:56.653 --> 00:02:59.653
(melancholic music)

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.240
- He looks like worn down by,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.780
presumably his one night
is spent by himself.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.665
- [Rev. Dave] Hey.

00:03:07.665 --> 00:03:08.498
- Dude.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.950
- I mean, the conditions that we're seeing

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.740
in the jail are so much
better than your average

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.290
like accused is going
to be in, in the U.S.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.400
- Well, they probably kept him

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.396
in solitary 'cause of the risk he posed.

00:03:19.396 --> 00:03:22.972
- [Crowd] Free Reverend
Dave! Free Reverend Dave!

00:03:22.972 --> 00:03:25.267
(Crowd chanting "Free Reverend Dave!")

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.832
- [Reporter] Reverend,
what do you have to say

00:03:26.832 --> 00:03:28.140
about being released today?

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.330
- I am grateful to the
court that they recognize

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.335
that this mandate was unconstitutional.

00:03:32.335 --> 00:03:34.410
(Devin sighs)

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:35.388
- Look, okay.

00:03:35.388 --> 00:03:37.110
Is it a mandate?

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.370
Is it a subpoena?

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.810
Is it something unrelated

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.570
that he actually was found
in contempt of court for?

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.200
Like, is the church protected
from criminal investigations?

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.505
Is it possible that they
might have to disclose things,

00:03:48.505 --> 00:03:50.550
you know, related to speech?

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.206
This is a really interesting exercise

00:03:52.206 --> 00:03:54.480
of the different parts
of the First Amendment.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.000
And I can't wait to see
how they resolve all

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.050
of these issues.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.940
- This is more of a human rights issue.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.620
It's about our right to seek truth

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.330
and to share that truth with others.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.160
- [Reporter] But considering
your church is associated

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.660
with a public university,

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.460
how do you define truth exactly?

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.222
- Okay. How is this church associated

00:04:11.222 --> 00:04:13.350
with a public university?

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.710
To the extent that there
is a formally recognized

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.570
or formally associated church

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:19.500
with a public university,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.450
that probably doesn't pass muster.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.670
But I gotta know the answer
to that question to be able

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.920
to understand anything else
that's going on in this movie.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:26.970
They'll tell, they'll tell us for sure.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.214
I completely trust them.

00:04:28.214 --> 00:04:29.047
(Devin laughs)

00:04:29.047 --> 00:04:29.880
- I'm sure.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:30.713
- Yeah.

00:04:30.713 --> 00:04:31.546
- Truth is a person,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.473
the person of Jesus Christ.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.250
It's the one truth above all others.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.390
- What do you have to
say to the people here

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.223
who are calling you,

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:42.673
- Jesus Christ is the one truth.

00:04:42.673 --> 00:04:43.890
Really?

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.780
This guy just can't help himself can he?

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.360
- A couple of things here,
not strictly speaking legal,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.380
but I'd like your take on this.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.213
Number one,

00:04:50.213 --> 00:04:51.420
the pastor gets outta jail

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.467
and the first question
he's presented with is:

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:54.925
- "How do you define truth exactly?"

00:04:54.925 --> 00:04:58.770
- Just a totally normal
question for a reporter to ask.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.757
- If only journalists would
corner people and say like,

00:05:00.757 --> 00:05:02.880
"Hey, what's your
epistemological framework?"

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.933
Or, you know, really getting
to the bottom of this stuff.

00:05:04.933 --> 00:05:05.940
- I would be on board with that.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:06.883
I think that's a great question

00:05:06.883 --> 00:05:07.716
to ask people.
- It'd be fun.

00:05:07.716 --> 00:05:10.410
I encourage any reporters
watching to start doing that.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.090
- No, it's a really weird thing to ask.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.140
- On top of it,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.370
this is a Christian pastor
somewhere in middle America,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.760
and he says probably
the most anodyne thing

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.940
I can possibly imagine a pastor saying.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.970
And it's national news, like,
'this guy can't help himself."

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.790
He's talking about religion.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:30.750
- There's a comment here.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.990
So we're looking at, I think
a fake Facebook stream.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:34.823
- [Devin] Yeah.

00:05:34.823 --> 00:05:35.977
- [Michael] And Brandy Lynn said:

00:05:35.977 --> 00:05:38.010
"What would you expect a pastor to say?"

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.909
Yes, Brandy.
- [Devin] Thank you.

00:05:39.909 --> 00:05:41.040
(Devin laughs)
- Like it's not,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.650
it's not super weird.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.940
- [Commentator #1] Right.
Freedom of worship.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.230
- In places of worship.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.150
But Hadleigh is a state university.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.440
- See and that's the question y'all.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.300
What's the church doing on a state campus

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.290
in the first place?

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:53.240
- That is a good question.
- It's a good question.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.020
- As the battle rages on in Hope Springs,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.400
- [Commentator #2] A historic landmark

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.600
that has served this community

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.960
for over a century and now
they want the church gone.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.393
This is what our country has come to.

00:06:05.032 --> 00:06:06.633
- The slow look back.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:08.700
- The Supreme Court,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.410
especially more recently,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.993
has allowed public funds to
flow to religious organizations,

00:06:14.993 --> 00:06:17.460
and especially has carved out exceptions

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.980
where there's long-standing traditions.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.650
The court tends to bend over
backwards to find an exception

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.300
that says it's not really religious,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.620
it's more about history.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.100
Maybe this church has
been on public grounds

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.363
for years and years.
- Yeah.

00:06:30.363 --> 00:06:33.690
Well maybe it predates the construction

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.800
of the campus around it.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:35.633
- Maybe.

00:06:35.633 --> 00:06:36.466
(glass shatters)

00:06:36.466 --> 00:06:37.380
(alarm rings)

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.213
- What was that?

00:06:38.213 --> 00:06:39.482
- [Rev. Jude] I don't know.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:42.315
(alarm continues)

00:06:43.385 --> 00:06:45.178
- [Rev. Dave] Hey! Hey!

00:06:45.178 --> 00:06:46.011
- [Rev. Jude] David!

00:06:46.011 --> 00:06:46.844
- [Rev. Dave] Get back here!

00:06:46.844 --> 00:06:48.358
- [Rev. Jude] David, no!

00:06:48.358 --> 00:06:50.689
(alarm continues)

00:06:50.689 --> 00:06:54.189
(melancholic piano music)

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.350
- This looks like
relatively petty vandalism.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:05.195
You got a kid who has spray
painted a single X on the sign

00:07:05.195 --> 00:07:09.420
and he has thrown a brick
through the window of a church.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.180
This appears to be religiously motivated.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:17.180
So there is technically a
federal anti-hate crime law

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.980
that does make it a federal crime to

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.123
destroy a religious property
on the basis of religion.

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.871
(object explodes)

00:07:27.871 --> 00:07:30.540
(alarm rings)

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.010
- Are we meant to believe the rock goes

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:37.010
through the window and then
magically hits the valve

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.630
on a gas pipe that's connected to nothing?

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.580
I mean, so here's one question.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:43.860
The person, the young
man, college student,

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.420
- Yes.
- presumably baseball player

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.950
who threw the brick through the window,

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:47.879
- right?
- Right.

00:07:47.879 --> 00:07:48.712
Right.

00:07:48.712 --> 00:07:50.580
- has inadvertently
presumably killed a man.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.413
- [Devin] Yes.

00:07:51.413 --> 00:07:53.588
- Would it be easy to get
him on murder for that?

00:07:53.588 --> 00:07:54.481
- No.

00:07:54.481 --> 00:07:55.314
- Okay.

00:07:55.314 --> 00:07:56.147
- No.

00:07:56.147 --> 00:07:58.380
Within the pantheon of
homicide, you have, you know

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:01.290
intentional murders where I literally

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:02.720
specifically intend to kill you.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:03.553
- Yeah.

00:08:03.553 --> 00:08:05.460
- Then you have things like manslaughter.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.730
Negligent homicide where someone dies

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.040
in the course of generally
something that is reckless

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.690
or inherently dangerous.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.880
There's the felony
murder rule where you are

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.470
committing an inherently dangerous crime

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:19.920
and during the commission
of that crime, someone dies.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.330
You are guilty of

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.163
- Yeah.

00:08:22.163 --> 00:08:23.700
- first degree murder
because you did something

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.680
that you should have
known would kill someone.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.486
Here he has thrown a
brick through window and

00:08:29.486 --> 00:08:34.170
through a Rube Goldberg series of events

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.960
an explosive light bulb went off

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.820
that combusted the rest of the gas.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.520
This kid doesn't have the
specific intent necessary

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.930
for murder

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:43.763
- Yeah.

00:08:43.763 --> 00:08:45.240
- or even the kind of negligence

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.100
that would be necessary
for a manslaughter.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.100
The law looks to what your intent was

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.160
in determining your level
of culpability or guilt.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.500
I do wonder though,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.090
could his next of kin,

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.530
the estate of Jude,

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:01.077
could they sue the
manufacturer of the light bulb

00:09:01.077 --> 00:09:04.740
or the manufacturer of the gas valve?

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.371
I mean, seemed to be some
major manufacturing defects

00:09:09.371 --> 00:09:12.330
that led to this Rube Goldberg of death.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.000
- Yes. Also seems like poor maintenance

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.610
on behalf of St. James Church.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.200
- If I was going to sue someone

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.630
on behalf of Jude or Jude's Estate,

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.150
I would probably sue the
light bulb manufacturer.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.670
I'd probably sue the
gas valve manufacturer.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:30.570
I'd probably sue the
church to get recompense

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.520
for this person that has died.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:33.621
- This is absurd.

00:09:33.621 --> 00:09:34.493
They can just take the church from us.

00:09:34.493 --> 00:09:36.510
I mean, don't we own this land?

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:37.343
- [Josh] Yes.

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:38.580
- So how is this legal?

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.050
- Eminent domain.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.210
I studied property law
a bit in grad school.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:43.043
As I understand it

00:09:43.043 --> 00:09:45.450
the state gave all public
universities complete autonomy

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.347
over eminent domain.

00:09:46.347 --> 00:09:49.050
- Can we study it a bit?
- Oh no.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.090
- Just a bit of property
law in grad school.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.497
- Wow. As one does.

00:09:52.497 --> 00:09:55.200
Okay. So we finally have an answer.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.050
What is the church's status
on this public university?

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.960
And the answer is it
has no status whatsoever

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.330
because it's not connected
in any way, shape, or form

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.738
because it's on its own
land that is just nearby

00:10:05.738 --> 00:10:07.980
to the public university.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.170
The church owns the land.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.220
This doesn't involve the
First Amendment in any way.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.430
Because the church owns this land,

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:19.560
the public university
is going to try to use

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.660
what's called eminent
domain where, you know,

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.769
a government entity can take over land

00:10:23.769 --> 00:10:26.250
to repurpose it for some other use.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.350
- Yeah. That's like when
houses get knocked down

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:29.310
to build a new freeway.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:30.540
- Exactly right. Exactly right.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:34.290
Now it is the case that sometimes

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:36.720
churches have been
subject to public domain.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.700
And it is also the case that

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.730
sometimes public universities
have more or less the power

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:44.010
to engage in eminent domain.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.430
The private entity is entitled
to the fair market value

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.470
of the property that has been taken.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.980
If the university had
a good faith, you know,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:56.610
non anti-religious reason for
taking over this property,

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.473
then it could probably do so.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:02.850
(Pearce sighs)

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:03.903
- Dad's church.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.163
- You can hardly even recognize it.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.620
- Hey look, this is a property law case.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.270
It's not really my thing.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.280
- [Rev. Dave] Pearce, you're
a social justice attorney.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:15.180
- Is that a thing?

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.290
- Theoretically, there
are civil rights lawyers.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.240
Social justice attorney?

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:22.920
I've never heard that term before.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.360
- Okay. Yeah, that's what I was wondering.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.552
- You would want someone who
specializes in property law.

00:11:26.552 --> 00:11:27.385
- Yeah.

00:11:27.385 --> 00:11:29.760
- Property law is very complex.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:30.750
There's a lot of ins and outs.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:31.583
You would want someone

00:11:31.583 --> 00:11:34.142
who has that built-in
institutional knowledge.

00:11:34.142 --> 00:11:36.360
You would definitely
not wanna hire this guy.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:37.830
- Well that's the classic
movie move, right?

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.390
That someone knows a lawyer,

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:40.555
they don't specify what their area is,

00:11:40.555 --> 00:11:41.388
- Yes.

00:11:41.388 --> 00:11:42.221
- And they're magically able

00:11:42.221 --> 00:11:43.200
to do the thing.
- Of course. Of course.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.033
Now I'm able to do that.

00:11:44.033 --> 00:11:46.170
- Yeah.
- But other people maybe not.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.370
- Let me get this straight.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.080
You drive all the way up here,

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.130
you don't even call.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.480
Were you afraid I'd say no?

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.720
- I didn't even know what
you'd say to be honest.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.640
Look, I have a little money.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:57.940
- I don't want your money.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.750
This is a legit offer.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:01.590
Thing goes to trial,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.800
jury will probably give you
half that amount. Less even.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.480
Money aside,

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.340
these things always get messy.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:09.930
- He just established
he doesn't know anything

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:11.220
about property law,

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.250
but he's willing to opine
that this is a good offer.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:15.240
You should take it.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.210
The jury might give you half of that, so.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:19.710
- Well, so for trial you're risking that

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:21.450
and you're paying for representation, so.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:22.283
- Yes.

00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:23.116
- Okay.
- Oh,

00:12:23.116 --> 00:12:23.949
If you go to trial,

00:12:23.949 --> 00:12:24.930
you're going to have to pay your attorney.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.670
It's very rare that
you get attorney's fees

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:27.503
from the other side.

00:12:27.503 --> 00:12:28.336
- Yeah.

00:12:28.336 --> 00:12:29.820
So you might spend hundreds of thousands

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:32.162
of dollars to get less
than hundreds of thousands

00:12:32.162 --> 00:12:35.184
of dollars back in an
increased amount of money.

00:12:35.184 --> 00:12:37.470
- God called me to fight.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:38.303
I'm gonna fight.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:41.680
- God calls you, you call me.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.870
- God called me to fight is,

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.820
has historically not worked out well.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.920
- There's, you know, in the New Testament

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.030
we refer to Christ as the Prince of Peace.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.330
So you could also take that route.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.830
There's like, "turn the other cheek."

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.690
There's a lot of evidence

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.780
that maybe the move isn't to do a holy war

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:03.630
but whatever Reverend Dave
thinks he needs to do.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:04.463
- [Pearce] Let me know.

00:13:04.463 --> 00:13:05.296
- [Rev. Dave] Yeah.

00:13:05.296 --> 00:13:06.634
- [Pearce] What, what's going on?

00:13:06.634 --> 00:13:07.467
- [Rev. Dave] They won't stop.

00:13:07.467 --> 00:13:08.670
Excuse me. Excuse me.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:10.140
Hey! Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.400
Stop, stop!

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.230
- I need you on the other
side of the tape, sir.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:14.340
- I'm the pastor of this church.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.140
- Well that may be, but it's not safe.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.490
- It's okay. I'm his attorney.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.773
- Congratulations and I'm a Mets fan.

00:13:19.773 --> 00:13:20.606
(Devin and Michael laugh)

00:13:20.606 --> 00:13:21.439
- What?

00:13:21.439 --> 00:13:23.520
- That's a great line. I like that.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.020
- Hey buddy, I have a demo permit.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.030
- You got a permit to wreck
the church on a Sunday. Really?

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:27.990
- Yeah. What can I say?

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.083
Signed by the president of the university.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:32.063
- [Michael] Wait.
- Okay.

00:13:32.063 --> 00:13:32.896
- I don't think the president

00:13:32.896 --> 00:13:33.900
of a university's ever gonna be able

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.400
to sign off on a demo permit.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.230
There's been absolutely no time

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:40.170
for the eminent domain process
to have worked at this point.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:43.320
Unless there is some ordinance that says

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.450
if there is a burned down building

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.160
it just has to be demolished.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.360
And that would have no effect

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:49.770
on the eminent domain thing

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.100
but have no effect on the
church's ability to rebuild.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.860
This is presumably I think
we have to believe it's

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.372
because this church is gonna
fall down and kill someone.

00:13:58.372 --> 00:14:00.372
- Yeah. And it looks like it's going to.

00:14:01.618 --> 00:14:04.285
(choir singing)

00:14:06.227 --> 00:14:08.810
- Your Honor, I am so sorry but

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:10.703
- Do not do this.

00:14:10.703 --> 00:14:12.540
This is a hundred percent improper.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:13.740
- in the middle of mass.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:15.473
- I'm aware.

00:14:15.473 --> 00:14:17.580
It's, it's very timely.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.680
It involves the church.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.360
- I have a signed injunction

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.880
from Judge Pascual that says, eh,

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.200
you can read it yourself.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.330
Cease and desist.

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:30.900
- If you talk to the judge
without opposing counsel present

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.820
generally that is a gross violation

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.640
of both court rules and ethics rules.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.110
That's an ex parte communication.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:38.430
So you can't do stuff

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.380
like this where you
talk to the judge alone

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:45.380
let alone on Sunday in a
church in a private setting.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:46.860
All of that's improper.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.770
Now there is a more fundamental problem

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:51.240
if you can even believe it

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.510
which is that there isn't a lawsuit here.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:55.440
Judges and lawyers,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.690
it's not like you can
just find a random judge

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:01.290
on the street and say, "Hey,
write an injunction for me."

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.570
Like there are limited situations where

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:06.063
you can get emergency ex parte relief.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:11.100
But even that takes 48 hours usually.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.017
Oh. And,

00:15:12.017 --> 00:15:13.230
(Devin laughs)

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.360
so an injunction is the thing that you get

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:16.980
at the end of a case.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:17.813
It is the,

00:15:17.813 --> 00:15:18.646
- [Michael] Oh,

00:15:18.646 --> 00:15:20.018
the final remedy that stops

00:15:20.018 --> 00:15:23.100
or forces someone to do something.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:25.350
- Man, now I have to find
a new film series to learn

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:26.280
about the law from.

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:27.720
- Guess so

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.760
- Barbara. We appreciate it.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.380
It's very courteous.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:32.213
I get it.

00:15:32.213 --> 00:15:34.290
It's a prime piece of land.

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.300
You know what I'd do if it were me?

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:39.960
I'd put in a big student center
with a fancy coffee shop.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.010
The kids would love it.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.890
I mean, to me that's more
valuable than a dusty old church.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:45.240
No offense.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:47.160
But he knows how I feel about it.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:50.610
Here's the problem, what you're doing,

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:52.530
it's against the law.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.990
'Cause eminent domain
doesn't automatically apply

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.190
just because you guys
don't like his church.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.553
So here's my counter.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.521
Withdraw your claim or we sue.

00:16:03.521 --> 00:16:06.900
- Whoa. A lawsuit over eminent domain.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:07.980
- We're done.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.660
- He's already laid the foundation

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.930
for why eminent domain
would apply in this case.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:17.610
The school is theoretically
not seizing this property

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:20.760
because of anti-religious furor

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:21.593
because they don't like the church.

00:16:21.593 --> 00:16:23.910
They're doing it because they
want to expand the campus.

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:25.590
So it's a question of valuation.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:27.270
Did they fairly compensate the church

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.680
for the land that they took?

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:29.610
- Josh you're a smart kid.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.710
Why'd you give up on law school?

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:33.489
- I wouldn't say I gave up on it.

00:16:33.489 --> 00:16:35.970
Once I spent time on a college campus

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:37.920
I realized there was a real need there.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.140
You know, ministry is just
another way for me to serve.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:41.460
- What kind of law were you studying?

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:42.300
- Same as you.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:43.890
Civil rights, social justice.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:46.390
- Really? 'Cause you don't
strike me as a liberal.

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:48.810
- Did he go to law school or not?

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.720
- Do you think that your
religious beliefs get

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.250
in the way of real social change?

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:55.740
- No, my, my beliefs are
the foundation of change.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.930
The belief that every human
is made in the image of God.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:01.260
I mean Jesus was the ultimate
social justice warrior.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.540
He said the lives of children mattered

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.400
and regarded women with honor and dignity

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.320
in a cultural moment
where a woman's testimony

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:09.660
wasn't even permissible in a court of law.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.220
- Well I've been doing this a long time

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.960
and I wish I'd seen those words put

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:14.220
into action more often.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:15.086
- Yeah, me too.

00:17:15.086 --> 00:17:16.860
But we're all responsible for our part.

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:18.960
But it's grace and then justice.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.123
- [Rev. Dave] Amen. Let's say grace.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.500
- He just wants eat his pizza

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:26.910
'cause cool guys love pizza.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:30.690
I like what Josh admits here,
but it's confusing, right?

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.320
'Cause he calls Jesus the
original social justice warrior.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:36.540
- But I have to wonder who that line

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:38.400
in this movie is geared for.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.500
Because the people I think

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:43.080
that enjoy these movies probably chafe

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:45.600
at the term social justice warrior

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:49.200
and to characterize Jesus
as a social justice warrior,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.970
the first social justice warrior,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:51.803
- Yeah.

00:17:51.803 --> 00:17:53.280
- seems like it's going to rub the people

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.050
that watch this movie the wrong way.

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:57.990
- So it's interesting to have
this like internal critique

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:59.040
within the film

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.920
'cause the films again are
representing this world

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.890
where it seems like
Christianity is about like laws,

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:09.840
institutions codifying beliefs
in the sort of objective way.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.460
And you have Cool Guy Josh saying no

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.350
it's about like living a life

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:15.960
of grace and justice and
all this sort of stuff, so.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.020
I mean, like you said, why is it there?

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:23.490
- At the same time, I think
it's the one little morsel

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:27.930
of an interesting read on
the function of religion.

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:31.320
- We condemn the hateful actions

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:36.320
of Adam Richardson in the
strongest possible terms.

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:39.930
We've taken the immediate
steps to expel Mr. Richardson

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:43.110
and we have encouraged
the state to prosecute him

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:45.900
to the fullest extent of the law.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:46.733
- Okay.

00:18:46.733 --> 00:18:47.566
- Hmm.

00:18:47.566 --> 00:18:48.450
- They have no power to do any of that.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:49.890
The church is on its own property.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.450
That's why they had to try to take it

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:55.380
in the first place for
through eminent domain.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.800
And then you can't make a recommendation

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.020
to the prosecutor to prosecute someone.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.037
Like you're just a stranger
to this whole thing.

00:19:03.037 --> 00:19:04.200
- Well it's like now

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:05.677
that it's convenient to him, he's like,

00:19:05.677 --> 00:19:06.840
"yes, the church

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:09.300
which we are a part of that
we own 'cause the university

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.070
and the church coexist
in a symbiotic way."

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:13.800
- You're expelled get off the
campus that you're not on.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:14.910
- Yeah.

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:15.743
- Ah!

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:18.030
- Not good.

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:19.860
Yeah. And I like how they
use that to make them look

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:21.060
like the good guys for a second.

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:23.430
But again, weird overlapping spheres.

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:24.263
- Man.

00:19:24.263 --> 00:19:25.231
- I'm so sorry.

00:19:25.231 --> 00:19:27.648
(Adam cries)

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:31.863
About Jude, I am so sorry.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:38.160
- I am withdrawing my
lawsuit against the school.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:39.510
- He's dropping the charges.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:41.133
- No, you don't get to do that.

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.940
The state prosecutes someone.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:47.250
The district attorney
will decide whether to

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:50.310
continue prosecuting someone or not.

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:51.630
A victim, even,

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.264
and he's a tangentially related victim.

00:19:54.264 --> 00:19:56.460
Yeah. They don't get to decide

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:58.500
whether charges are dropped or not.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.240
- Yeah. It seems like the
logic he's using there is like

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.370
if someone comes into my
house and kills my friend

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:03.900
I can be like, "no, no, it's my house"

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:04.733
- [Devin] Yeah.

00:20:04.733 --> 00:20:05.566
- "so I'd like to drop the charges."

00:20:05.566 --> 00:20:06.547
- "Yeah, no, just don't prosecute him."

00:20:06.547 --> 00:20:08.467
"I have forgiven him."

00:20:08.467 --> 00:20:10.380
"He can go free now."

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.260
- We just ended up where
we were in the beginning.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.480
We took a long journey to get

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.910
to him saying like,
"Fine, take the church."

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:19.530
- This is the most self-defeating movie

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:20.850
I think I've ever watched.

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:23.820
He just used legal services
of his cool guy brother

